A NOTE ON WHAT WAS NOT USED. A Thai pixel font would have been the neater
answer, and there are several. The closest fit found was TA Chai Lai_Pixel by
Taweechai Assawrangsithaeng (https://www.f0nt.com/release/ta-chai-lai-pixel/),
which is offered free for personal, non-commercial use, with commercial use by
paid written permission. This repository is published on GitHub, so shipping
that font would be redistributing it, which its terms do not clearly allow —
and "probably fine" is not a licence. Two OFL faces and a hundred lines of
span code is the cost of not having to make that call.

No Microsoft font, icon or other asset is used anywhere in this app. The
window chrome is layer-list shapes in this repository and the icons are
vectors drawn from squares; see res/values/colors_retro.xml.

OpenStreetMap, through Nominatim — the name over the weather window
-------------------------------------------------------------------
The kiosk sends the broker its own coarse position, rounded to two decimals,
and the broker turns that into a province name with Nominatim's reverse
geocoder (https://nominatim.openstreetmap.org). Nothing is redistributed here:
no OSM data is stored in this repository or shipped in the APK. What is used is
the service, one lookup per square kilometre the kiosk has stopped in, cached
for a day.

    Data © OpenStreetMap contributors, available under the Open Database
    Licence (ODbL): https://www.openstreetmap.org/copyright

That attribution is carried in the dashboard payload as the "place" credit
rather than left in somebody's memory, which is the same rule Open-Meteo's
CC BY 4.0 attribution follows.

Nominatim's usage policy (https://operations.osmfoundation.org/policies/nominatim/)
asks for a real User-Agent identifying the application, no more than one request
a second, and no bulk geocoding. All three are met by construction: the
User-Agent names this repository, the cache means a kiosk that does not move
asks once a day, and a kiosk that does move asks once per kilometre.

CoinGecko — which coins are the largest
----------------------------------------
The market-capitalisation ranking behind the four coins on the screen comes
from CoinGecko's public API (https://www.coingecko.com/en/api), asked once a
day: about 30 calls a month against a free tier of 10,000. No key is sent and
nothing is redistributed. The PRICES are Binance's, as they were before —
CoinGecko is asked which coins, never what they cost.


SFace — recognising Poom's face (0.37.0)
----------------------------------------
app/src/main/assets/models/face_recognition_sface_2021dec_int8.onnx comes from
OpenCV Zoo, models/face_recognition_sface:
https://github.com/opencv/opencv_zoo/tree/main/models/face_recognition_sface

    sha256 2b0e941e6f16cc048c20aee0c8e31f569118f65d702914540f7bfdc14048d78a
    (matches the Git LFS pointer in OpenCV Zoo)

That directory states: "All files in this directory are licensed under Apache
2.0 License." The licence text is licenses/SFACE-LICENSE. The model is a
MobileFaceNet trained with the SFace loss by Yaoyao Zhong
(https://github.com/zhongyy/SFace, paper arXiv:2205.12010), converted to ONNX
by Chengrui Wang.

WORTH KNOWING: the original SFace repository carries no licence of its own, and
the paper trains on CASIA-WebFace, VGGFace2 and MS-Celeb-1M, face datasets
released for research. The Apache-2.0 grant is OpenCV Zoo's. This project is a
personal, non-commercial home kiosk (see LICENSE), which is the use those
datasets allow; if that ever changes, this model is the second thing to review
after openWakeWord's.

It runs on the phone with ONNX Runtime, which the wake word already ships. No
face image or embedding ever leaves the phone.

ML Kit face detection — finding the face and the blink (0.37.0)
---------------------------------------------------------------
com.google.mlkit:face-detection (bundled model, on-device), under the ML Kit
Terms of Service: https://developers.google.com/ml-kit/terms. It finds the face,
its five landmarks for alignment, and the eye-open probability for the blink.
Nothing is sent to Google by it.

Google Play services auth — Google Drive's access token (0.61.0)
----------------------------------------------------------------
com.google.android.gms:play-services-auth (AuthorizationClient), under the
Android Software Development Kit License: https://developer.android.com/studio/terms.
It asks Google Play services on the phone for a Drive access token; the Drive
calls themselves are this project's own code (drive/DriveApi.kt).

CameraX — the front camera (0.37.0)
-----------------------------------
androidx.camera, Apache-2.0. Open only while a verification or an enrolment is
on screen; frames are analysed in memory and never written.

Thai word segmentation for the voice (broker, 0.38)
---------------------------------------------------
server/kiosk_broker/data/words_th.txt is PyThaiNLP's Thai word list, copied
from pythainlp 5.3.7 (pythainlp/corpus/words_th.txt, 62,102 words,
sha256 4bf0ab93a570d91b33ae7bbf627eddd5a56f81c6ba33a94c3950f38904235bda).
PyThaiNLP's corpus_license.md lists it among the word lists "created by the
PyThaiNLP project and released under Creative Commons Zero 1.0 Universal
Public Domain Dedication License": https://github.com/PyThaiNLP/pythainlp

It is read by nlpo3 (https://github.com/PyThaiNLP/nlpo3, Apache-2.0), installed
into the broker's virtualenv by install.sh, not redistributed here. The
project's own additions are server/tts_words.txt. Both decide only where the
words are in the text sent to the voice.

smbj — the file manager's NAS, read only (0.44.0)
-------------------------------------------------
com.hierynomus:smbj 0.14.0 (https://github.com/hierynomus/smbj), Apache-2.0.
SMB 2/3 in pure Java: no native code. It brings, all pure Java:

    org.bouncycastle:bcprov-jdk18on 1.79   Bouncy Castle Licence (MIT-style),
                                           https://www.bouncycastle.org/licence.html
    com.hierynomus:asn-one 0.6.0           Apache-2.0
    net.engio:mbassador 1.3.0              MIT
    org.slf4j:slf4j-api 2.0.9              MIT. No SLF4J provider is shipped, so
                                           smbj writes no log at all: nothing it
                                           knows about the NAS reaches logcat.

BouncyCastle's post-quantum data files (org/bouncycastle/pqc/**) and its
certificate-path message files are left out of the APK (app/build.gradle.kts,
packaging); SMB uses neither.


LibVLC - the second player (0.60.0)
-----------------------------------
LibVLC 3.7.6 from VideoLAN (https://www.videolan.org/vlc/libvlc.html), GNU LGPL
version 2.1 or later; the full text is licenses/LGPL-2.1.txt, and a copy is in
the APK at assets/licenses/LGPL-2.1.txt. Built by this project, arm64 only, with
no GPL component: VLC's contribs with --disable-gpl, and every VLC module that
declares the GPL left out (tools/libvlc/build-lgpl.sh). The source commits, the
build, the license report of every module and library in it, and how to build
it again are in licenses/LIBVLC.md. It is fetched by its pinned SHA-256 and is
not kept in git. The libraries it bundles keep their own licences (FFmpeg
under the LGPL 2.1; others BSD, MIT, zlib and similar), listed in the report.
It is used only for files Media3 cannot play (media/PlayerChoice).
